# work-folder.env: the one place that says where the port's work folder and the PC's lock files are. No other file
# of the repository writes these paths (scripts/structure_check.py --explain 18); pages may name them.
#   a shell script   . port/setup/work-folder.env   then W=${PORT_WORK:-$PORT_WORK_DEFAULT} and, for the locks,
#                    L=${PORT_LOCKS:-$PORT_LOCKS_DEFAULT}; [ -d "$L" ] || L=$W   (wslpath -w gives Windows' spelling)
#   Python           port/setup/work_folder.py reads this file: WORK_CHECKOUT, WORK_DEFAULT, LOCKS_DEFAULT
# The environment decides first, as ever: PORT_WORK names another work folder (port/setup/setup.sh makes one),
# PORT_LOCKS another folder for the lock files (TIMING.lock, BUILD.wanted.*), which are in one place for the whole
# PC. By default both are build/work in this checkout (PORT_WORK_CHECKOUT; the repository ignores build/), as a
# build folder is in any project's clone. The Windows build needs it on a Windows drive (/mnt/<drive>/..., no
# spaces): cmd.exe cannot work in a folder of WSL's own filesystem, so a checkout there names another folder. A PC
# names its own folders in ~/.config/piconet/work-folder.env, lines NAME=/mnt/<drive>/path outside every checkout,
# which the last line reads over these.
# The one exception: the CMake superbuild cannot read this file, so CMakeLists.txt's PORT_WORK default says build/work
# in the checkout too (the same folder; the port's scripts always give it PORT_WORK from here).
# Only comments and the four lines below: the busy check sources this file, and work_folder.py refuses anything else.
PORT_WORK_CHECKOUT=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)/build/work
PORT_WORK_DEFAULT=$PORT_WORK_CHECKOUT
PORT_LOCKS_DEFAULT=$PORT_WORK_CHECKOUT
[ ! -f "${HOME:-}/.config/piconet/work-folder.env" ] || . "${HOME:-}/.config/piconet/work-folder.env"
